{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability in the Crusader states. He inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Due to his youth a regency was established under his mother and later under his uncle Baldwin III of Jerusalem. During this period the County of Tripoli was vulnerable to attacks from Muslim forces and internal power struggles. Raymond III was known for his cautious approach to warfare and diplomacy which sometimes drew criticism from more aggressive nobles.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a key figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem after the death of King Amalric I in 1174. He was appointed regent for the young King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. As regent Raymond faced the rising threat of Saladin who was uniting Muslim forces in Syria and Egypt. Raymond tried to negotiate peace with Saladin and avoided unnecessary battles. His diplomacy was often seen as weak by his peers who favored bold military action. He was eventually removed from the regency in 1176 by a faction led by the Knights Templar and his rival Guy of Lusignan. Despite this setback Raymond remained a powerful noble and continued to influence court politics.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a central role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He opposed Guy of Lusignan’s decision to march the army to relieve Tiberias. Raymond warned that the plan would lead to disaster due to lack of water and poor terrain. His advice was ignored and the Crusader army was crushed at Hattin. After the battle Raymond retreated to Tripoli and prepared its defenses. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of 